## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The correlated stopped-train theorem now gives the global endpoint/interface floor

\[
J_D:=(b-P_F)+(Q+4f),
\qquad
\liminf {J_D\over D}\ge j_*:=2\rho+4\sqrt{\Delta_2}-\pi+0.00084,
\tag{CI.1}
\]

where

\[
\rho=\cos\rho,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
j_*=2.146668840296357\ldots .
\]

This note propagates (CI.1) into canonical Section-85/77 currencies. The resulting
bounds are substantially stronger than the older train-interface baseline and expose
an additional live obstruction: every full-core survivor carries large *local chamber
packing slack* even though a direct comparison with the single full-hull Oler slack is
not valid.

No C-II closure is claimed.

## 1. The smaller Section-85 interface currency

Put

\[
H_D:=(b-P_F)+(Q+2f).
\tag{CI.2}
\]

Canonical Section 85 uses `H_D`, while the full stopped-train handshake naturally
produces `J_D`. Since

\[
J_D-H_D=2f
\]

and `Q>=f`, `b-P_F>=0`, one has the finite inequality

\[
\begin{aligned}
5H_D-3J_D
&=2\bigl[(b-P_F)+(Q-f)\bigr]\\
&\ge0.
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{H_D\ge {3\over5}J_D.}
\tag{CI.3}
\]

Combining with (CI.1),

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {H_D\over D}
\ge h_*:={3\over5}j_*
=1.288001304177814\ldots .
}
\tag{CI.4}
\]

This sharpens the old Section-85 use of only
`B_4-P_F>=4sqrt(R_4)-P_F` by retaining the all-train correlation with the loaded hull.

## 2. A much stronger second-order positive-core population floor

Canonical Section 63 gives

\[
b+Q=2(q-k),
\tag{CI.5}
\]

and Section 67 gives

\[
m=R_4+f+q-k+1.
\tag{CI.6}
\]

Hence (CI.2) has the exact finite interpretation

\[
\begin{aligned}
H_D
&=b-P_F+Q+2f\\
&=2(q-k+f)-P_F\\
&=2(m-R_4-1)-P_F.
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
m-R_4={P_F+H_D\over2}+1.}
\tag{CI.7}
\]

In the canonical full-core branch `P_F/D->pi`, so (CI.4) gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {m-R_4\over D}
\ge c_{pop}:={\pi\over2}+{3j_*\over10}
=2.214796978883804\ldots .
}
\tag{CI.8}
\]

For comparison, canonical Section 85 alone records only

\[
\liminf {m-R_4\over D}\ge\sqrt{\Delta_2}=0.9523\ldots .
\]

The new coefficient is more than twice as large.

## 3. Chamber area surplus becomes explicit and large

Canonical Section 85 proves

\[
A_{ch}-a_0Z_{ch}\ge c_0H_D,
\qquad
a_0={\sqrt3\over2},
\qquad
c_0={\sqrt3\over4}={a_0\over2}.
\tag{CI.9}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {A_{ch}-a_0Z_{ch}\over D}
\ge \beta_*:={3c_0\over5}j_*
=0.557720924762737\ldots .
}
\tag{CI.10}
\]

Since `Z_ch>=0`, this also gives the weaker but sometimes useful geometric statement

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {A_{ch}\over D}\ge\beta_*.
}
\tag{CI.11}
\]

The result is only boundary-order. It does not contradict the full-core condition,
which excludes quadratic inactive area but permits linear thin-corridor area.

## 4. Total inactive-chamber perimeter has the full `J_D` floor

Let `P_ch` be the total perimeter of the inactive chambers from canonical Section 77.
The exceptional bounded faces contribute exactly

\[
P_{exc}=4f+Q.
\tag{CI.12}
\]

If `a` is the number of convex outer pockets, Section 77 gives

\[
P_{out}=b+P_F-2q+2a.
\tag{CI.13}
\]

Every side of the loaded force hull joins distinct unit-separated source points, so
its length is at least one. Hence

\[
P_F\ge q.
\tag{CI.14}
\]

Subtracting `b-P_F` from (CI.13),

\[
P_{out}-(b-P_F)=2(P_F-q+a)\ge0.
\tag{CI.15}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
P_{ch}=P_{exc}+P_{out}\ge (4f+Q)+(b-P_F)=J_D.
}
\tag{CI.16}
\]

Together with (CI.1),

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {P_{ch}\over D}\ge j_*
=2.146668840296357\ldots .
}
\tag{CI.17}
\]

This is an explicit lower bound complementary to Section 77's coarse upper bound
`P_ch<=13 pi D`.

## 5. The chamber Oler-slack sum is forced above `1.717 D`

For each inactive chamber `C_j`, canonical Section 77 defines the isolate-only Oler
slack

\[
\sigma_j={A_j\over a_0}+{P_j\over2}+1-z_j\ge0.
\]

Let

\[
\Sigma_{ch}:=\sum_j\sigma_j,
\qquad
s:=\#\{\text{inactive chambers}\}.
\]

Summing the definitions gives the exact identity

\[
\Sigma_{ch}
={A_{ch}-a_0Z_{ch}\over a_0}+{P_{ch}\over2}+s.
\tag{CI.18}
\]

By (CI.9), `c_0/a_0=1/2`, (CI.3), and (CI.16),

\[
\begin{aligned}
\Sigma_{ch}
&\ge {H_D\over2}+{J_D\over2}\\
&\ge {3J_D\over10}+{J_D\over2}
={4J_D\over5}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\Sigma_{ch}\over D}
\ge {4\over5}j_*
=1.717335072237086\ldots .
}
\tag{CI.19}
\]

This coefficient is larger than `pi/2`. That observation is **not** a contradiction.
The single full-source Oler slack on `K=conv(A)` does not dominate the sum of chamber
slacks: partitioning creates internal chamber perimeter, and `P_ch/2` appears explicitly
in (CI.18). Any closure using (CI.19) must cancel or globally pay that internal-boundary
term rather than compare the two slacks naively.

## 6. A linear deficit from the absolute rhombus-count maximum

The full-source Oler bound in canonical Section 77 is

\[
n\le \Delta_2D^2+{\pi\over2}D+1.
\tag{CI.20}
\]

Since `m<=n`, (CI.8) implies

\[
\Delta_2D^2-R_4
\ge(m-R_4)-{\pi\over2}D-1.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\Delta_2D^2-R_4\over D}
\ge \gamma_*:={3j_*\over10}
=0.644000652088907\ldots .
}
\tag{CI.21}
\]

This is compatible with `R_4/D^2->Delta_2`; it supplies a second-order linear gap,
not a leading-density contradiction.

Multiplying by `a_0` gives the equivalent geometric deficit

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\pi D^2/4-a_0R_4\over D}
\ge a_0\gamma_*=\beta_*
=0.557720924762737\ldots .
}
\tag{CI.22}
\]

Indeed

\[
{\pi D^2\over4}-a_0R_4
=\delta_K+[\operatorname{area}(K)-A_F]+A_{ch}+E_4,
\tag{CI.23}
\]

where all four terms are nonnegative. Equation (CI.10) already forces the chamber
part at the same numerical scale, so (CI.22) is best viewed as a useful count/area
consistency check rather than an independent closure.

## 7. Frontier effect

The correlated all-train theorem no longer leaves Section 85 at the small
`4sqrt(Delta_2)-pi` interface scale. Every full-core survivor now has simultaneously

```text
J_D / D >= 2.1466688402... - o(1),
H_D / D >= 1.2880013041... - o(1),
(m-R_4) / D >= 2.2147969788... - o(1),
(A_ch-a_0 Z_ch) / D >= 0.5577209247... - o(1),
P_ch / D >= 2.1466688402... - o(1),
Sigma_ch / D >= 1.7173350722... - o(1),
(Delta_2 D^2-R_4) / D >= 0.6440006520... - o(1).
```

The cleanest new closure target is now the chamber-slack identity (CI.18). A theorem
that pays a fixed positive fraction of the internal chamber perimeter from the single
full-source boundary budget would immediately turn the large local slack floor into a
global obstruction. Without such a perimeter cancellation, comparing local and global
Oler slacks is invalid.

A second possible target is the linear rhombus-count deficit (CI.21): couple it to
canonical Section 68's punctured-packing identity and the phase/angular-defect budget
at **linear** order, rather than only through `o(D^2)` rhombic saturation.

The macroscopic-container branch is untouched.

## 8. Scope audit

- All statements are full-core Type-II only.
- `b` is the canonical occurrence-counted old outer contact-face walk.
- `P_F` is Euclidean force-hull perimeter; `P_F>=q` uses only unit separation of
  consecutive force-hull vertices.
- No local exceptional-face perimeter is identified with the Section-123 induced-cell
  quantity `sum_r P_exc,r`; those are different ledgers.
- Contact-isolated source points remain in the source and enter `Z_ch` exactly as in
  canonical Section 77.
- The `.00042` / `.00084` margin is proof-only pending independent audit of the new
  sharpened §168 artifact.
- No source-preserving deformation, exact unit triangle, or Level-I conclusion is
  asserted.
